function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    print("<div class=\"doc-detail-layout\"><main class=\"content doc-page doc-detail\"><div class=\"doc-title\"><h2>COMPONENT</h2></div><div class=\"doc-section signatures\"><h3>Capy</h3><pre>function COMPONENT(request : dval)\nfunction COMPONENT:NAME(request : dval)</pre></div><div class=\"doc-section content\"><p><code>COMPONENT</code> marks a function as a renderable fragment. Another unit reaches it with <code>component()</code> or <code>component_render()</code>; it is never routed to directly by a URL.</p><p>The handler takes one parameter, <code>request</code>, of type <code>dval</code>, and returns nothing. Everything it <code>print</code>s becomes the fragment&#39;s output.</p><h2>Plain and named handlers</h2><p>A file with a single fragment defines <code>COMPONENT</code>. Callers name just the file:</p><p>component_render(&quot;lib/card.capy&quot;)</p><p>A file that holds several related fragments gives each one a name, and callers select it with a <code>:</code> suffix:</p><p>component_render(&quot;lib/widgets.capy:CARD&quot;)</p><p>A caller inside that same file can use the bare suffix form, <code>component_render(&quot;:CARD&quot;)</code>, which resolves to the current file.</p><p>Names are matched exactly and are conventionally uppercase.</p><h2>Reading input</h2><p>Caller-supplied props arrive as <code>request.props</code>. Read them through <code>string()</code>, <code>s64()</code>, and the other converters so a missing key has a defined value:</p><p>var title := string(request.props.title, {fallback: &quot;Untitled&quot;})</p><p><code>request.props</code> is swapped in for the duration of the call and restored when the handler returns, so a component never disturbs its caller&#39;s props, and nested components each read their own. The rest of <code>request</code> — query, f")
    print("orm, headers, session — is the live request and is shared with the caller.</p><h2>Lifecycle</h2><p>The first time a unit is used in a request, its <code>ONCE</code> handler runs before the component handler. A component may render other components; each nested call resolves relative paths against its own file.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section\"><h3>Errors</h3><p>A caller that names a component Bearer cannot resolve gets a <code>500</code> status and a banner element in place of the fragment. The handler in this file never runs. Callers that treat a fragment as optional should guard with <code>length(component_resolve(name)) &gt; 0</code>.</p></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">print(string(request.props.name, {fallback: &quot;guest&quot;}))\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">var title := string(request.props.title, {fallback: &quot;Untitled&quot;})\nvar count := s64(request.props.count)\nprint(&quot;&lt;article&gt;&lt;h3&gt;&quot;, title, &quot;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;&quot;, count, &quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/article&gt;&quot;)\n</pre></div></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">print(&quot;&lt;section&gt;&quot;)\ncomponent_render(&quot;/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD&quot;, {title: string(request.props.title, {fallback: &quot;Untitled&quot;})})\nprint(&quot;&lt;/section&gt;&quot;)\n</pre></div></div></main><aside class=\"doc-section see-also\"><h3>See also</h3><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/component/\">component</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/component-render/\">component_render</a><")
    print("a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/component-resolve/\">component_resolve</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/handler/render/\">RENDER</a></aside></div>")
}
